.\" Generated from the command definitions by `make docs`; do not edit.
.TH DROOT 1 "" "droot" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
droot \- a super\-easy container with chroot, without docker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B droot
[\fIglobal options\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIARG\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
droot exports the file system of a container image as a tar archive (export), runs a command inside an extracted archive with chroot(2) (run), as root or rootless in a user namespace, cleans up the mounts made by run (umount) and shows them (status), and runs commands in the user namespace of rootless runs (unshare).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.PP
Accepted before and after \fICOMMAND\fR.
.TP
\fB\-D, \-\-debug\fR
enable debug output [$DROOT_DEBUG]
.TP
\fB\-q, \-\-quiet\fR
print errors only: no warnings, informational messages nor progress [$DROOT_QUIET]
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
format of the messages on STDERR: text or json (one log/slog JSON object per line) [$DROOT_LOG_FORMAT]
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.TP
\fB\-v, \-\-version\fR
print the version
.SH COMMANDS
.SS droot export [options] IMAGE
.PP
Export the file system of a container image as a tar archive.
.PP
Writes the file system of IMAGE as a tar archive, with the image's configuration in /.droot/config.json and its environment in /.drootenv, for 'droot run'.
.PP
IMAGE is an image of a Docker daemon (NAME[:TAG], an ID, ...), or one of these images, read without daemon:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
docker://[REGISTRY/]NAME[:TAG|@DIGEST]  an image of a registry (Docker Hub by default)
docker\-archive:FILE[:NAME:TAG]          an archive written by docker save
oci:DIR[:NAME]                          an OCI image layout
oci\-archive:FILE[:NAME]                 a tar archive of an OCI image layout
.RE
.fi
.PP
From a daemon, droot creates a temporary container from IMAGE (without starting it), streams its file system and removes the container. The daemon is selected like the docker CLI does, from DOCKER_HOST, DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY, DOCKER_CERT_PATH, DOCKER_CONTEXT or the current docker context.
.PP
Without daemon, droot applies the layers of the image itself, verifying their digests. The credentials for registries are those of the docker CLI (docker login); the layers are downloaded to $TMPDIR (default /tmp) for the export.
.TP
\fB\-o,\fR \fI\-\-output FILE\fR
write the archive to FILE instead of STDOUT (written atomically)
.TP
\fB\-\-platform\fR \fIPLATFORM\fR
export the PLATFORM variant of a multi\-platform image, e.g. linux/arm64 (default: the platform of the daemon, or the one droot runs on)
.TP
\fB\-\-pull\fR
pull IMAGE (for \-\-platform) from its registry into the Docker daemon first, with the credentials of the docker CLI
.TP
\fB\-\-compress\fR \fIALGO\fR
compress the archive with gzip, or zstd (with the zstd command)
.TP
\fB\-\-checksum\fR
also write the SHA\-256 checksum of the archive to FILE.sha256 (sha256sum format; needs \-o)
.TP
\fB\-\-reproducible\fR
make the archive depend only on the image: modification times later than $SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH or the image's creation time are set to it
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
leave out the files matching PATTERN and everything below matching directories; a PATTERN with a / matches absolute paths (/usr/share/doc), one without a / base names (*.pyc) (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-drop\-docker\-files\fR
leave out the files Docker adds to every container: /.dockerenv and the empty /etc/hostname, /etc/hosts, /etc/resolv.conf and /dev/console
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SS droot run [options] [\-\-] [COMMAND [ARG...]]
.PP
Run a command in a container root directory with chroot(2).
.PP
Mounts /proc, /sys and the requested bind mounts under ROOT_DIR, creates the basic device files, chroots into ROOT_DIR, drops capabilities from the bounding set, switches to \-\-user/\-\-group and executes COMMAND with the environment saved by 'droot export' plus \-\-env\-file and \-\-env.
.PP
Without COMMAND, the ENTRYPOINT and CMD of the image saved by 'droot export' (/.droot/config.json) are run; COMMAND replaces both. The image's USER and WORKDIR are the defaults of \-\-user and \-\-workdir.
.PP
The options must come before COMMAND: everything from COMMAND on is passed to it verbatim. Mounts are left in place after COMMAND exits; remove them with 'droot umount'.
.PP
Run by another user than root, droot runs rootless: it runs again as root of a new user namespace, where your user is root and your subordinate IDs (/etc/subuid and /etc/subgid, with the newuidmap and newgidmap commands) are the other users, and waits for COMMAND, passing signals on to it. The mounts are private (as with \-\-unshare), /proc and the device files are bind mounts of the host's. Extract archives with 'droot unshare' so that their files keep their owners.
.PP
Under systemd, COMMAND gets the notification socket (NOTIFY_SOCKET, for Type=notify), the sockets of socket activation (LISTEN_FDS, LISTEN_PID, LISTEN_FDNAMES) and the watchdog (WATCHDOG_USEC, WATCHDOG_PID) of droot, which \-\-env and \-\-env\-file override. A NOTIFY_SOCKET that is a path is bind mounted read\-only on the same path in the container. Rootless, the notifications come from a child of droot, the main process of the service: set NotifyAccess=all in the unit.
.PP
\-\-config FILE reads options from FILE, in a subset of TOML: lines KEY = VALUE, where KEY is the long name of an option and VALUE a string ("..." or '...'), true or false, or an array of strings for repeatable options; command, an array of strings, gives COMMAND. # starts a comment. The options given on the command line take precedence, and repeatable ones are added to the file's:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
root = "/var/containers/app/current"
unshare = true
bind = ["/var/log/app", "/srv/data:/data:ro"]
command = ["/app/bin/server", "\-\-port=8080"]
.RE
.fi
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
read options from FILE: the long names of the options of droot run as keys, and command, in a subset of TOML (see droot(1)); the command line takes precedence
.TP
\fB\-r,\fR \fI\-\-root ROOT_DIR\fR
root directory to chroot into (required)
.TP
\fB\-u,\fR \fI\-\-user USER\fR
user (name or ID) to run COMMAND as (default: the image's USER, or droot's own user); names are looked up in ROOT_DIR/etc/passwd, then on the host
.TP
\fB\-g,\fR \fI\-\-group GROUP\fR
group (name or ID) to run COMMAND as (default: the primary group of \-\-user)
.TP
\fB\-w,\fR \fI\-\-workdir DIR\fR
working directory of COMMAND inside the container (default: the image's WORKDIR, or /)
.TP
\fB\-b,\fR \fI\-\-bind SRC[:DEST][:OPTS]\fR
bind mount SRC from the host on DEST (default: SRC) in the container; OPTS is a comma\-separated list of ro, rw and rbind (recursive) (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-robind\fR \fISRC[:DEST][:OPTS]\fR
read\-only bind mount (same as \-\-bind SRC:DEST:ro) (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpfs\fR \fIPATH[:OPTS]\fR
mount a tmpfs on PATH in the container; OPTS are mount flags (ro, exec, ...: nosuid,nodev,noexec by default) and tmpfs options (size=64m, mode=1777, ...) (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-copy\-files, \-\-cp\fR
copy the host's /etc/group, /etc/passwd, /etc/resolv.conf, /etc/hosts into the container
.TP
\fB\-\-bind\-net\-files\fR
read\-only bind mount the host's /etc/resolv.conf and /etc/hosts into the container, so that COMMAND sees later changes made to them in place (unlike \-\-cp)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-dropcaps\fR
do not drop capabilities (dangerous)
.TP
\fB\-\-cap\-add\fR \fICAP\fR
also keep the capability CAP (e.g. NET_RAW, SYS_PTRACE, or ALL) in the bounding set (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-cap\-drop\fR \fICAP\fR
drop the capability CAP (e.g. CHOWN, or ALL) from the ones kept by default (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-ulimit\fR \fINAME=SOFT[:HARD]\fR
set a resource limit of COMMAND, e.g. nofile=65536 or core=0:unlimited (names of ulimit, as in docker run) (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-unshare\fR
make the mounts in a private mount namespace: they disappear when COMMAND and its children exit, and need no 'droot umount' (always the case rootless)
.TP
\fB\-\-unshare\-ipc\fR
run COMMAND in a private IPC namespace (System V IPC, POSIX message queues)
.TP
\fB\-\-hostname\fR \fINAME\fR
run COMMAND in a private UTS namespace with the host name NAME
.TP
\fB\-\-read\-only\fR
make the root directory read\-only for COMMAND (implies \-\-unshare); use \-\-tmpfs or \-\-bind for the paths it writes
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-mask\fR
do not mask the sensitive files of /proc and /sys nor make /proc/sys, /sys and the like read\-only (dangerous)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-new\-privileges\fR
set no_new_privs: setuid programs and file capabilities cannot grant privileges to COMMAND (default: true; \-\-no\-new\-privileges=false lets programs such as sudo or su work)
.TP
\fB\-\-seccomp\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
seccomp profile: default (deny the system calls Docker's default profile denies, such as unshare, mount, keyctl, bpf; amd64, arm64, arm and 386) or unconfined (the default)
.TP
\fB\-e,\fR \fI\-\-env KEY[=VALUE]\fR
set an environment variable, overriding /.drootenv and \-\-env\-file; a bare KEY takes its value from droot's own environment (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-env\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
read KEY=VALUE lines from the host's FILE, overriding /.drootenv; a bare KEY takes its value from droot's own environment (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SS droot umount [options]
.PP
Unmount everything mounted under a root directory by 'droot run'.
.PP
Lazily unmounts (MNT_DETACH) every mount point below ROOT_DIR, deepest first, and fails if some cannot be unmounted. ROOT_DIR itself is never unmounted. It is safe to run when nothing is mounted.
.TP
\fB\-r,\fR \fI\-\-root ROOT_DIR\fR
root directory used with 'droot run' (required)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SS droot status [options]
.PP
Show the mounts and the processes of a root directory.
.PP
Lists the mount points below ROOT_DIR, i.e. the mounts made by 'droot run' that 'droot umount' would remove, and the processes whose root directory is ROOT_DIR: the commands started by 'droot run' and their children.
.PP
Processes of a 'droot run \-\-unshare' are listed, but their mounts are in a private mount namespace and are not. Run as root to see every process.
.TP
\fB\-r,\fR \fI\-\-root ROOT_DIR\fR
root directory used with 'droot run' (required)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SS droot unshare [COMMAND [ARG...]]
.PP
Run a command as root of the user namespace of rootless runs.
.PP
For users other than root. Runs COMMAND (default: $SHELL, or /bin/sh) as root in a user namespace with the ID mapping of 'droot run' without root: your user as root, and your subordinate IDs (/etc/subuid and /etc/subgid, with the newuidmap and newgidmap commands) as the other users. There, files keep the owners of an archive, so that the users of an image can write their files:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
droot unshare tar \-xpf app.tar \-C app
.RE
.fi
.PP
Files owned by subordinate IDs can only be deleted in the namespace too:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
droot unshare rm \-rf app
.RE
.fi
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SS droot completion bash|zsh|fish
.PP
Print a shell completion script (bash, zsh, fish).
.PP
Prints a script that completes the commands and options of droot.
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
bash:  source <(droot completion bash)            # e.g. in ~/.bashrc
zsh:   source <(droot completion zsh)             # e.g. in ~/.zshrc
fish:  droot completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/droot.fish
.RE
.fi
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DROOT_DEBUG, DROOT_QUIET, DROOT_LOG_FORMAT
Defaults of \-\-debug, \-\-quiet and \-\-log\-format.
.TP
.B DOCKER_HOST
Address of the Docker daemon used by droot export: unix:///path, tcp://host:port, https://host:port or ssh://[user@]host[:port].
.TP
.B DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY, DOCKER_TLS, DOCKER_CERT_PATH
TLS for tcp:// hosts, verifying the daemon's certificate or not, with ca.pem, cert.pem and key.pem from DOCKER_CERT_PATH (default ~/.docker).
.TP
.B DOCKER_CONTEXT, DOCKER_CONFIG
The docker context to use, and the directory of the docker CLI's configuration (default ~/.docker), which holds the current context and the registry credentials used by droot export \-\-pull.
.TP
.B DOCKER_API_VERSION
Pins the version of the Docker Engine API.
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Time (in seconds since the epoch) to which droot export \-\-reproducible clamps modification times.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
https://github.com/yuuki/droot
